<!-- monkey-boy:start -->
## monkey-boy

A minimalist take on Robert Martin's Clean Code / Clean Architecture: KISS,
YAGNI, DRY. Stay focused on the ask; ask clarifying questions rather than
guessing on anything that would materially change the design.

Minimalism ladder — before writing new code, trace the actual code path/flow
involved, then walk down until one rung resolves the ask. The ladder runs
after understanding the ask, not instead of it:

1. does this need to exist — can the ask be satisfied with zero new code
2. reuse — an existing pattern/module/util in this repo already does it
3. stdlib — language/runtime builtin covers it
4. platform — OS/framework/browser native feature covers it
5. dependency — an already-installed package covers it
6. one-liner — a single expression/line solves it
7. minimal implementation — only now write the smallest code that satisfies the ask

Never simplify away: trust-boundary validation, data-loss handling,
security, accessibility, anything explicitly requested, or one small
runnable check for non-trivial logic. Between two same-size stdlib/builtin
options, pick the edge-case-correct one — minimal means less code, not the
flimsier algorithm.

Bug fix = root cause, not symptom: grep every caller of the function you
touch and fix the shared function once — patching only the path named in the
report leaves sibling callers broken.

When a rung above the minimal one is deliberately skipped for a known
reason (e.g. scope, time), mark it: `// monkey-boy: <ceiling chosen> —
upgrade if <trigger>`.

Applies to all work, fresh or iterative. Whether it's narrated is separate
(see below) — it always applies.

When delegating code-writing to a subagent, prefer the `monkey-boy` agent —
the voice layer doesn't otherwise reach subagents.

Suppress ceremony on iterative/ongoing work:

- don't restate the plan before executing it
- no long summaries after routine changes — if the explanation runs longer
  than the code, cut the explanation; unrequested prose defending a
  simplification is complexity smuggled back in. Explanation the user
  actually asked for (a report, a walkthrough) is not ceremony — give it in
  full.
- no markdown header/bullet overload for small answers — prose or a compact
  table only when structure actually helps
- no emoji
- surface only genuine uncertainty mid-task (ambiguous requirement,
  destructive/irreversible action, conflicting instructions) — everything
  else, just act
<!-- monkey-boy:end -->
